+## Change-accounting verdict
+
+Do not approve §K's blanket “none” as written. The file's draft status is not an exemption from A9: A9 incorporates an ENCODING-V1 signing baseline and registers C4/C5/C6 meanings and the object_format outcomes. A later unregistered profile cannot override those by relabeling a change as wording.
+
+The owner can make many new completion choices without an amendment: a concrete container, an independent trust-config mechanism, explicit key transport, exact DNSSEC algorithms, a body encoding, and a frozen catalog. These are **new owner choices**, not automatically existing-rule CLARIFYs. Restrictions on wildcards, OTS operations/attestations, signature subpackets, canonical transport, git headers and repository algorithms should be visible as supported-path limits. None by itself demonstrates that protection available within A9's model has been conceded.
+
+There is no established unavoidable NARROW-MODEL finding. There are two conditional author surfaces: excluding a channel-capture path because config authorization is shared (F04), and weakening an actual pre-issue commitment claim to a numeric chain-time comparison (F01). Implement the missing authorization boundary or correct the claim explicitly; do not announce that either issue has already disappeared. G05 requires a registered reading, not an invented cryptographic security claim about all-branch coverage.
+
+“No signed byte changes” is only defensible as **no mandatory attestation-frame layout change for these resolutions**. Freezing edited spec/vector objects changes their signed hashes; converting the DNS body changes its anchored S preimage and needs a fresh stamp; trust configuration gets a new detached signature; signature policies can change allowed signed signature output. Existing archived proof bytes cannot be converted in place while retaining their stamp. The authorized hybrid supplement still changes slot encoding and requires its own vectors. Existing fallback P8 golden/rejection expectations need not be rewritten merely to add above-codec policies, but boundary and full-profile vectors remain necessary.
+
+## Verification evidence and limits
+
+- `PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 python3 formal/p8/vectors/check.py`: implementation B reported **71/71 golden round-trips, 173/173 same-code rejections, 5/5 negative controls**. This verifies the current codec suite, not any proposed full verifier.
+- An in-memory script under `prototype/.venv/bin/python` imported implementation A, loaded SIG-01, set bit 7 on the unhashed type-16 subpacket, and verified with the vector public point: **codec accepted, mathematical verification True**. No mutated vector file was written. A first attempt with system Python lacked rfc8785; the existing prototype environment supplied it, with no installation.
+- Read-only traversal using local python-opentimestamps over `timestamps/**/*.ots` and the two upgraded spike `out/*.ots` files found **127 files, 344 Bitcoin attestations, 500 pending attestations**; operations were only append/prepend/SHA256, and all 344 Bitcoin paths' first consecutive SHA256 pair had a **125-byte input**. The draft reports 126 files/496 pending: the current corpus has one additional pending-only file. The format-support assertion for the observed Bitcoin paths is corroborated; general future-calendar support is not established.
+- Prototype `spike/verify_offline.py:249–276` does mark each insufficient-header attestation unperformable while selecting lowest good height, confirming the G05 instrument departure. `real/ots.py:92–95` selects lowest merkle-correct height but by itself does not enforce depth/pins; the draft's complete qualification rule is stronger than that helper.
+- `prototype/spike/issue.py` emits indented JSON, STMT_DIGEST and the old frame-only preimage. Its archived output cannot substantiate “the issue path always emits JCS” for the new container. It is a prior instrument, not evidence that G17 rejects an already conforming shipped V1 receipt.
+- Primary standards checked online: [RFC 9580](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9580.html), [RFC 4034](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4034.html), [RFC 4035](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4035.html), [RFC 1982](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1982.html), and [RFC 9718](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9718.html). RFC 1982 leaves the half-range ordering undefined; the profile must choose an outcome. The live IANA XML tool fetch failed, so no claim is made about today's file containing particular optional public-key elements.
+- No formal model was run, no real full-profile forged bundle was issued, and no C-row, preregistered gate or implementation readiness was discharged. Counterexamples above distinguish semantic rules and assumptions from successful attacks on an implemented V1 verifier.
